<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsar was the title of the supreme ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Caesar, formally adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> in 1547. It expressed claims to imperial status and to succession from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> later added the title of Emperor, but tsar remained in common use until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1917, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> fought several wars against the tsars of Muscovy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsar.jpg" alt="Tsar" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse Mucha · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Car_Simeon_Bulharsky_-_Alfons_Mucha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 10th century, under <b>Tsar</b> Peter I, the priest Bogomil founded a dualist religious movement, the Bogomils, which rejected the church hierarchy and the material world and later spread to Byzantium and western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1610, after the victory at Klushino, Polish troops occupied Moscow and a group of boyars elected the Polish prince Władysław as <b>Tsar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan IV ('the Terrible'), who reigned from 1547 to 1584, was crowned <b>Tsar</b> and began the eastward expansion beyond the Ural Mountains into Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol attacks from 1242 forced the tsars to pay tribute, and in 1277 the swineherd Ivailo led a peasant rising that beat off Mongol raiders and killed <b>Tsar</b> Constantine Tikh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 January 1547 his grandson Ivan IV, then sixteen, was crowned <b>Tsar</b> of All Russia by Metropolitan Macarius in the Dormition Cathedral of the Moscow Kremlin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many officers were at first encouraged by the early liberal reforms of <b>Tsar</b> Alexander I, when liberalism was officially encouraged and expectations ran high.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li></ul></section>
